---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import QuoteForm from "../../components/QuoteForm.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../config";

const title = "Keratin Treatment for Color-Treated Hair";
const description =
  "Keratin treatments on color-treated hair — timing coloring and keratin correctly, protecting your color, and what to ask your stylist first.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/keratin-for-color-treated-hair/">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10 prose-section">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-600 font-medium mb-2">
      <a href="/" class="hover:underline">Keratin Treatment Elmwood Park</a> / Keratin for Color-Treated Hair
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-serif font-bold text-brand-900 mb-5">
      Keratin Treatment for Color-Treated Hair
    </h1>
    <p>
      Keratin treatments and hair color aren't incompatible, but the order and timing matter more than most clients
      expect. Get it wrong and you can end up with faded, uneven color or a keratin treatment that doesn't take
      evenly. Here's how to sequence the two correctly.
    </p>

    <h2>Which Should Come First: Color or Keratin?</h2>
    <p>
      As a general rule, most stylists recommend <strong>coloring first, then keratin</strong> — with roughly a
      one- to two-week gap in between. Coloring involves opening the hair cuticle to deposit or lift pigment, and a
      keratin treatment then seals the cuticle back down, which can actually help lock color in and extend its
      vibrancy. Doing it in the reverse order — keratin, then color shortly after — risks the coloring process
      re-opening the cuticle and prematurely stripping out some of the keratin treatment you just paid for.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you're coloring and getting keratin in the same visit or very close together, tell your stylist explicitly
      so they can sequence the appointment (or space out two appointments) correctly. This is one of the most common
      questions worth asking directly during a consultation, since the right order can depend on the specific
      products being used.
    </p>

    <h2>Will Keratin Fade My Color?</h2>
    <p>
      Done in the right order, a keratin treatment is more likely to help color last longer than to fade it, because
      it smooths and partially seals the cuticle, reducing how much color washes out with each shampoo. The bigger
      threat to color longevity after either service is the same thing that shortens keratin results: sulfate
      shampoos and frequent hot-water washing. A sulfate-free routine protects both your color and your keratin
      treatment at once — see our <a href="/keratin-aftercare/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">aftercare guide →</a>
      for specifics.
    </p>

    <h2>Permanent, Semi-Permanent, and Demi-Permanent Color</h2>
    <p>
      The type of color you use affects how much you need to worry about interaction with keratin. Permanent color
      that's already processed and settled is generally the most stable going into a keratin appointment.
      Semi-permanent and demi-permanent color sit closer to the hair's surface and can be more sensitive to the heat
      and chemistry of a keratin treatment, which is another reason a short gap between the two services helps —
      it gives the color time to fully set before it's exposed to a flat iron at keratin-treatment temperatures.
    </p>

    <h2>If Your Color-Treated Hair Is Already Damaged</h2>
    <p>
      Repeated coloring, especially lightening or bleaching, can leave hair more porous and prone to over-processing
      if it's then hit with a strong chemical smoothing treatment. If your color history includes bleach or frequent
      lightening, a gentler, formaldehyde-free option is often the safer starting point rather than a full-strength
      traditional keratin service. See our
      <a href="/formaldehyde-free-keratin/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">formaldehyde-free keratin guide →</a>
      or ask specifically about <a href="/cysteine-treatment/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">cysteine treatments →</a>,
      which tend to be gentler on already-processed hair.
    </p>

    <h2>What to Tell Your Stylist</h2>
    <p>
      Before booking, be ready to share: how recently you colored, whether it was permanent or semi/demi-permanent,
      whether any bleaching or lightening was involved, and how your hair has responded to chemical services in the
      past. A stylist working from full information can sequence your appointments and pick the right formula instead
      of guessing.
    </p>

    <p>
      Want the full rundown on what to expect afterward regardless of your color history? See the
      <a href="/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">keratin treatment overview →</a> or
      <a href={`tel:${SITE.phone}`} class="text-brand-600 font-medium">call {SITE.phoneDisplay}</a> to talk through
      your specific color and hair history before booking.
    </p>
  </div>
  <section class="max-w-md mx-auto px-4 pb-16">
    <QuoteForm compact />
  </section>
</Layout>
